    "id": "prop:no-terminal-effect-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If \\(D(0)=D(1)\\) almost surely, then every \\(\\nu_x\\in\\mathcal N_x\\) satisfies \\(\\nu_x(D(0)>t,R(1)\\le r)=\\mu_{1x}(D(1)>t,R(1)\\le r)\\), so \\(\\Theta_I(P)\\) is a singleton equal to the ordinary standardized difference of within-arm cumulative incidences among the common survivor cohort.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "D(z)",
      "\\nu_x",
      "\\mathcal N_x",
      "R(z)",
      "\\mu_{zx}",
      "\\Theta_I(P)",
      "P"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:iff-capacity-projection",
      "ass:terminal-monotonicity"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction checks that the capacity theorem collapses to the familiar identified survivor contrast when treatment has no terminal effect.",
    "gap": "This is a required sanity reduction rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "It supplies a diagnostic implementation test and interpretable benchmark for applied users.",
    "proof_tex": "Assume the displayed no-terminal-effect premise as a restriction on the compatible full laws.  Then the two identified death margins coincide.  For any \\(\\nu_x\\in\\mathcal N_x\\), define finite measures on the readmission space by\n\\[\n q_{x,t}(B)=\\nu_x((t,\\infty]\\times B),\n \\qquad\n k_{x,t}(B)=\\mu_{1x}(D(1)>t,R(1)\\in B).\n\\]\nThe capacity restriction says \\(q_{x,t}(B)\\le k_{x,t}(B)\\) for every Borel \\(B\\).  Equality of the death margins gives\n\\[\n q_{x,t}(\\mathcal E)=d_x((t,\\infty])\n =\\Pr(D(1)>t\\mid X=x)=k_{x,t}(\\mathcal E).\n\\]\nApplying the capacity inequality to \\(B^c\\) yields\n\\[\n q_{x,t}(B)=q_{x,t}(\\mathcal E)-q_{x,t}(B^c)\n \\ge k_{x,t}(\\mathcal E)-k_{x,t}(B^c)=k_{x,t}(B).\n\\]\nThus \\(q_{x,t}=k_{x,t}\\), and in particular\n\\[\n \\nu_x(D(0)>t,R(1)\\le r)\n =\\mu_{1x}(D(1)>t,R(1)\\le r).\n\\]\nThe control term \\(b_x(r,t)\\), the denominator \\(S(t)\\), and both within-arm measures are identified from \\(P\\) by the IPCW lemma.  Substitution into the TV surface formula leaves no dependence on \\(\\nu\\); integration leaves no dependence in the RM surface either.  The capacity-projection theorem says every compatible surface is represented by some \\(\\nu\\in\\prod_x\\mathcal N_x\\), so \\(\\Theta_I(P)\\) is the singleton ordinary standardized within-arm cumulative-incidence contrast among the common survivor cohort.  This proves point identification from the premise; it does not define point identification into existence.",
